    "knowledge_point": "Obtaining a Higher-Order Derivative Of a Function Given its Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial",
    "problem": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=1+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{64}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{‴}(0)?$",
    "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{‴}(0)}{3!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{‴}(0)}{3!} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{‴}(0)}{6} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
